<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy - App Store privacy manifest.

  package_app.sh copies this to Contents/Resources/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy (bundle
  Resources ROOT - Apple does not look anywhere else). It lives here beside the
  entitlements plists rather than in data/ because data/ is copied wholesale with
  `cp -R` into Resources/data/, which would seal a duplicate into the signature,
  and because data/ is for assets the app LOADS at runtime - this file is
  packaging metadata the app never reads.

  Every declaration below was derived from the actual imports of the built mas
  binary (`nm -u build-mas/chipmachine`), not from guesswork. Everything is
  statically linked, so vendored engines count as our own code. Re-check with:

      nm -u build-mas/chipmachine | grep -E '_stat|_statfs|_getattrlist|mach_absolute_time'

  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty and that is accurate: the app has no
  analytics, no crash reporter, no account system, and sends nothing about the
  user off-device. It fetches music from public archives over HTTP; that is
  outbound content retrieval, not collection of data ABOUT the user.

  UserDefaults is deliberately NOT declared: the app has no NSUserDefaults or
  CFPreferences use at all (settings live in its own config file under
  Application Support, via Environment::setAppName()). Confirmed by the absence
  of _OBJC_CLASS_$_NSUserDefaults and CFPreferences* from the binary's imports.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>

    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <!-- Binary imports _stat, _fstat, _lstat, _getattrlist.
             C617.1 - metadata of files inside the app container: the music.db
                       index, the web cache and the song cache under
                       ~/Library/{Caches,Application Support}/ (sandbox container
                       on mas), which the indexer stats to decide what is stale.
             3B52.1 - metadata of files the user specifically granted access to:
                       tunes opened by double-click or the open panel, reached
                       through the security-scoped bookmarks in
                       FileOpenHandler.mm. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>C617.1</string>
                <string>3B52.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!-- DiskSpace is deliberately NOT declared, despite the binary importing
             _statfs / _fstatfs. Nothing in the shipped binary reads a free-space
             field. Checked, and worth re-checking if this ever changes:
               * sqlite3 (libsqlite3.a) calls statfs/fstatfs only to read
                 f_fstypename and MNT_RDONLY, to choose a file-locking strategy
                 - see sqlite3.c around lines 37795, 38500, 39905. It never
                 touches f_bavail/f_bfree.
               * JUCE (libsksplugin.a / libBaseCli.a / libBaseExport.a) does have
                 a real free-space query in juce_SharedCode_posix.h:636, but it
                 is DEAD-STRIPPED - no getBytesFreeOnVolume symbol survives in
                 the linked binary. Verify with:
                     nm build-mas/chipmachine | c++filt | grep -i BytesFree
             The app also does not behave differently when disk space is low, so
             E174.1 would be a false attestation, and it displays no disk-space
             information, so 85F4.1 does not apply either. No accurate reason
             code exists, so no reason is claimed.

             If App Store Review returns ITMS-91053 (missing API declaration) for
             NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace, do NOT paper over it with
             E174.1. The fix is either to keep the free-space code out of the
             link (it already is) and appeal with the evidence above, or to add a
             genuine low-disk behaviour and then declare E174.1 truthfully.
-->
        <!-- Binary imports _mach_absolute_time, used for audio/frame timing and
             elapsed-time measurement between in-app events. That is exactly
             35F9.1. No absolute event timestamps leave the device. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>35F9.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
